A||||23||Flamberge||(Long) Fiero/Firebird X-Files||||3||
Z||000000||Flamberge||10-09-2001||05:01 AM||||I hope this hasn't been covered, but here is my theory about General Motors and the mysterious deaths (murders?) of the Fiero, and now the Firebird... (This is mostly my opinion and observation so proceed with caution.)<P>Fact: When the Fiero came out, it redefined Pontiac's image, and mapped that division's course through 2002 (but no later.) (After all, "we build excitement!" was the ad campaign launched with the Fiero itself, due to one of its original names - Fiamma - italian for "flame, or excitement.)<P>- The "excitement" was well deserved, as all of you will agree with me. (Though I do admit that the 84 base model had some growing pains, it was still lightyears ahead of anything like it at the time.)<P>Each year the Fiero got better, getting tons of publicity and advertising dollars. It was even an Indy Pace car. (Look at a same-year MR2 or CRX....looks like junk on wheels next to a Fiero.)<P>Meanwhile, Pontiac's other sports car, the venerable Fierbird, was still seeing reletively strong sales (partly still due to "Smokey and the Bandit, despite it being an earlier (2nd) gen model and now older movie,) mostly due to Knight Rider. <P>By 1988, however, Firebird sales were beginning to wane. So were Fiero sales. Fiero sales may have been due to the early recalls, or to the unforgiving media. (Remember the "Suzuki Samurai?")<P>I think Pontiac made the decision (or GM made it for them) to cut production of the Fiero to bolster Firebird sales. More on this in a minute, but I wonder if someone here who actually remembers 1988 better than I do could lend me your knowledge. (I was in 8th grade in 88, and didn't really know anything baout Fieros, certainly not enough to follow public release info, trade mags, etc.)<P>Skip to the Firebird, 4th Gen, present day. Some would/will argue with me, but I think they are the best looking production models that GM has ever made. (Fieros are up there too.) Why has the Firebird "failed" in today's marketplace? <P>I believe its wings were clipped.<P>Here's why: Performance figures for the '98 -'01 Firebird Formulas (which I choose to example here because they are faster than the heavier Trans Ams) have been purposefully hidden from the public. Hot Rod magazine, in its May 1998 issue had an entire article on how the F-body (Firebird/Camaro) performance figures were misrepresented versus the Vette. The reason? Because you do not want to have a 40 grand car that can't outrun a 25 grand car that has the same engine, tranny, etc.<P>Hot Rod went on to say "So what we appear to have are slightly overrated Corvettes and grossly underrated F-bodies." <P>It is no secret that the Corvette is faster, through a more clog-free breathing system, better gear ratios, etc. I can understand their trepidation at advertising the Vette's closer-than-we-realise performance envelope, but I also believe it is one of the key factors at killing the F-body.<P>This is not a slam on Corvettes. C-5s ROCK, but a Firebird Formula, freed of its GM restrictions, can be just as fast, IMO.<P>Another reason for the death of the Firebird is the lack of a decent ad campaign. When was the last time you saw a strictly Firebird ad? For me it was 1999. In Pontiac publications it went longer, with the "this car isn't for everyone" campaign, which is a horrible way to sell anything. <P>"This is a good vacuum cleaner, but not for everyone, probably not for you, as a matter of fact." What is that, reverse psychology?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>If you think the advewrtising thing isn't important, think of the current (and very well done) Mitsu Eclipse commercials.<P>They also haven't been adequately represented at dealerships in years. Is this due to tired sales or a bigger GM plan? It knew it would retire the F-body as early as 1997 (*Anchorage Alaska Pontiac GMC Dealership) and therefore probably let sales slip as it pulled units out of dealers, or slowed down delivery. Maybe the sales did it, or it caused the poor sales. Is the tail wagging the dog? <P>Go to any Pontiac dealership since 1998 (the last incarnation of the Firebird) and how many birds do you see? Maybe three, if it is serving a large city. Then go to a Ford dealership. How many Mustangs do they have? Fifteen? Twenty? <P>I don't know what GM - and so Pontiac - will do in the near future to further dig themselves a hole, but I think retiring the Fiero in 88 *after only four years on the market* was a disastrous move, one that they are still paying for. The styling of the Fiero was years ahead of its time. When I brought my 88 GT home for the first time, my neighbor thought it was less than 2 years old! It is 13! (It's styling is 15!!!) <P>So maybe GM retired the Fiero to make way for the Firebird, to pick up sales they knew would begin to slump, and/or maybe it was political. Who knows for sure. I believe it is something sinister, though, not so innocent as "sales were bad" since they only gave the car four years to prove itself. Not very long for a brand new model. The Fiero sold well over those four years, not as good as GM would have liked, obviously, but I bet it has done better than the *involuntary gag reflex* Aztec. If they used Fiero-tactics in dealing with the Aztec, it wouldn't have lasted four days on the public scene. It is also an indication of where Pontiac is going, and fast. (Hint: it's a pretty hot place.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> )<P>To show it hasn't learned from its mistake, Pontiac (GM) is now killing the Firebird. Pontiac will now only really have the Grand Prix as the "excitement" in the line up. Don't get me wrong, my wife drives a 95 Grand Am, but it isn't sporty, not like a Firebird or Fiero. <P>And don't get me started on the Sunfire. (Anyone seen the show car version? Could they possibly have injected it with any more valium before releasing it to the public?)<P>I believe that the Fiero, and now the Firebird, are being killed off to make way for GM's bigger agendas, be they SUVs, ugly all-in-one Aztec monstrosities, or other plastic-clad bricks (like the Avalanche.) It is a sad thing, to have a car that drives like a Fiero, looks like a Fiero, has the personality of a Fiero, and yet only survives four years. Then to turn around and kill two staples of Americana (F-bodies) just because of "lagging sales" and an obselete Canadian plant, (St. Therese.)<P>What could the Fiero have been? What would it look like now? (2002?) I for one love the 89 Fiero prototype. A nice blend of Fiero-ness and Firebird-ness. My two favorite cars.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I welcome all comments, and this might have been covered before. I don't want to start a flame war, and feel too new here to really make any waves, so I hope everyone reads this with a grain of salt. I have nothing against Pontiac, I just hope changes are made to the Division and to GM that will revive its sagging spirit. I have owned 3 Fieros, a Grand Am, a 69 Malibu (my first car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> and two Subarus (looong story.) I prefer Pontiac as a brand name, I just hope they get their act together. Comments? Thanks.<P>Epiloque - "We build excitement" was true, now they build the Aztec, a minivan (!) and are killing the Firebird like the Fiero before it. <P>- Flamberge||||reg||3
Z||000001||My7Fieros||10-09-2001||05:11 AM||||Good God thats a long post lol...ok I got about 1/3 the way through it so forgive if you mentioned this....<BR>This has been discussed zillions of times on here...some times people have even posted mag articles on the reason for the demise of the Fiero.<BR>I think that most of us agree for the most part that it was several factors.....some being the obvious bad publicity it got for the fire recalls, dropping sales every year since 84 (btw the 84 Fiero was the biggest selling Pontiac to date), and, possibly most of all, the testing of both a turbo v6 and a v8 project for possible future Fieros had the "powers that be" at Chevy saying NO NO...they didnt want any in house compititon against the Vette.<BR>Thing is, try to find a Pontiac or any GM big wig thats <I>not</I> trying hard to forget the Fiero.....wierd stuff, for sure.<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="blue"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Master_Sushi||10-09-2001||11:02 AM||||Firebird/Camaro is WAY pastdue for a new setup. They have been the same since when? The 70s or so? lol Don't get me wrong they are great cars. I drive a 99 firebird very often, and love it!<BR>GM should quickly redesign the f-bodys WHILE still selling the old ones, and do it right. <P>Most likely the new fbody will be some crappy hybrid front wheel drive automatic transmission V6 based on the sunfire or something. haha<P>The mustang sells better because its more modern, its been updated and ford markets its way more. One of GM's bigest faults is its marketing, whoever is in charge of marketing should be SHOT! Some of the GM stuff you see on TV and the adds you read in mags. God you'd think either a retard thought them up or a child. Or both.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Ford is going to overtake GM as the bigest american car division in the next few years. I dont like ford at all, but im starting to dislike GM more. Infact I sometimes almost hope they go under.||||reg||13
Z||000003||LZeitgeist||10-09-2001||11:30 AM||||Good post. two things jump out at me, though - <P>1) - The Fiero was in productions for five years, not four. The model years of 1984, 1985, 1986, 1987 and 1988.<P>2) F-Bodies have been due for a rework for quite a while. I'm sure the Firebird will come back around again. As long as it stays rear-wheel drive (and doesn't get raped like the Pontiac LeMans marque did), I don't much care what they do to the design, since anything decent that's newer than my Formula is out of my price range anyway.<P>Just my two cents.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||falconhulk||10-09-2001||12:05 PM||||Why are they due for a rework???  The statements you make about them being the same since the 70's are aimed aty the engine still being pushrod?  Thats the major complaint in the car rags!  Hmmm a older technology engine that puses 340 HP (New camaro ann edition)  AND gets 30 mpg????  Do we use automatic hammers (well yes in some fields but you know what I mean)?  If it gets the job done why put it down?  The ford engine is the only thing that has developed on the stang.  Sure it a mod dual overhead cam but is it still not up to the output of the fbodies?  My firebird is so stable through turns it is second only to my fiero.  The engine is High output the susp. is great so why change it?  I could undersrnd a revamp as in previous generations.  This is the 4th gen firebird.  The mustang is also in its 4th gen.  Make no mistake put a 70's bird against a new formula and you will see how much they have changed.  The new birds will beat every stock bird or camaro ever made.  Think the new stangs will beat every other year stang?  In handleing only.  They are not just upgrading the platform like was done for all other generations they are STOPPING production.  There have been leaks about a new camaro being made but nothing on a firebird.    I have also heard talk of combineing these two sports car line into one and while economical it kills a legacy.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lou_dias||10-09-2001||12:20 PM||||Uh, LS1's aren't very durable.<BR>Lots of bent pushrods.<P>then there's the clutch that doesn't last 50k miles...  It's getting to be a Fiero-like warranty nightmare.<P>My little brother just had a dealer rebuild his 98 Trans AM engine.  Bad bearings...rod knock...<P>You don't hear about this in the "vette" community because who's going to beat on a Vette like a Camaro or Firebird...||||reg||1
Z||000006||LZeitgeist||10-09-2001||12:28 PM||||When I speak of 'rework', I speak of style, not function. GM will always have engines to put in their cars, no matter what they look like. Engine design and specs do not concern me (within reason), as I don't buy cars for the thing under the hood. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroMontreal||10-09-2001||01:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Flamberge:<BR><B>Then to turn around and kill two staples of Americana (F-bodies) just because of "lagging sales" and an obselete Canadian plant, (St. Therese.)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Ugh, Why do I feel some hatred on your behalf... don't you think that "Obsolete Canadian Plant" is kinda harsh? Our plant is pretty damn sweet.. lol.. We have invested a ****  load of money in their F-body plant but GM just doesn't wanna do it's part.. Very nice, up-to-date plant with good workers... and a crappy paint shop.. lol oh well.. every good thing comes to an end ..(Fiero 84-88)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Flamberge||10-09-2001||01:58 PM||||Sorry FieroMontreal!! I didn't mean to imply that the St. Therese plant is bad, I was saying it from GM's perspective. *They* are the ones who claim it is obselete, and would rather let the F-bodies - and the plant itself - fold, than try to rebuild both. I for one have nothing against the St. Therese plant, I really don't. They have been building my favorite car for years and years. (On a side note, I have only been to Canada in the form of Cold Lake Air Base - but the chow hall had the BEST steak and I got my incentive ride (F-15D) there, so Canada harbors many fond memories for me.) Sorry again. I was saying that sarcastically, the way GM would have said it. Sorry.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>With all due respect<BR>- Flamberge||||reg||9
Z||000009||falconhulk||10-09-2001||02:43 PM||||The current generation of fbodies have the best sports car engine GM has ever made.  It very efficent and despite some claims its durable.  It also has a VERY big aftermarket at the moment.  You can build up the engine for half the price of a fiero v8 swap and blow away the competition at the track.||||reg||1
Z||000010||TI_3VOM||10-09-2001||03:11 PM||||...a few odds and ends...<BR>1) There's rumor/speculation about the Camaro (1/2 of the F-body stable) to come back in 2004 as a retro Camaro (about '67ish looking). No word on a replacement/return of the Firebird.<BR>2) What is it about the GM mentality that says that in order to be successful, 100K units must be sold per year, profits be damned. (again, Corvette excluded)<BR>3) Speaking about profits, does anyone have articles/interviews/magazines with proof that the Fiero made money every year it was made?<BR>4) Is it me, or am I seeing a slow, quiet turning of Pontiac from the performance division into ????<BR>5) (again, Corvette excluded) Why is GM so adverse ir producing niche vehicles? (aside from spreading the costs out with different cars on the same frame)<BR>6) Why not more than one sports car in GM? Some one looking at a Miata or MR-2 isn't necessarliy in the market for a Corvette. Why couldn't the Fiero fill this void? <P>....enough rants for now.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Master_Sushi||10-09-2001||03:49 PM||||When I said they are basicly the same design as in the 70s I ment a few things. Mostly crapy suspension, solid rear axel. Body has flex in it. They have looked the same for years and years now. To much body overhang. They are basicly an old car thats been minorly updated over the years.The engines in them now are great, even the 3800 is pretty quick. I am in no way insulting them(that I can see anyway) , I love f bodys. Infact I would probably drive an 80s fbody now If I didnt get into fieros.<P>I think with a redo they could be even better cars. But fact is GM will probably fungle it up like they allways do.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000012||Raydar||10-09-2001||05:25 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TI_3VOM:<BR><B>...a few odds and ends...<BR>There's rumor/speculation about the Camaro (1/2 of the F-body stable) to come back in 2004 as a retro Camaro (about '67ish looking). No word on a replacement/return of the Firebird.<P>Why not more than one sports car in GM? Some one looking at a Miata or MR-2 isn't necessarliy in the market for a Corvette. Why couldn't the Fiero fill this void?<BR>... <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>One rumor regarding the Camaro is that it will be based on the Australian Holden platform. Specifically, the new Holden Monaro.<BR>This would <I>not</I> be a bad thing. Similar Holdens have LS1 V-8s, six speeds, and rear wheel drive. In a car about the size of a Grand Prix.<P>I have also heard (read) a rumor that Pontiac is considering the production of a car at the Bowling Green facility. (I'm going to keep on repeating this one until it happens, or until someone convinces me that it wont.) Corvettes and the new Cadillac roadster are already "there".<BR>Why not a 2 seat Pontiac? Could happen. Pontiac definitely <I>needs</I> a new performance flagship. The GP is a nice car, but it ain't it.<P>Time will tell.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||1
