Z||000022||Rick 88||10-13-2001||12:33 AM||||Here in the southwest Fieros sold very well. In fact at our dealership in 1987 the Fiero averaged 18% of total Pontiac monthly sales. We used to stock 60 to 80 Fieros on the lot at all times. Maybe it was the mild winters here, or the more affordable (compared to Firebird) insurance rates. <P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000023||Flamberge||10-13-2001||02:20 AM||||I looked at the sales figures for all five (and not four hehe) years the Fiero was made, and it averaged out to 70,000 cars sold a year, give or take. (I don't have the sheet in front of me, but the 75 years of pontiac is the book I got the stats from.) Even though the numbers did fall every year, they were comparable to the Firebird until 87 and 88. 88 dropped to roughly 27,000 sold, but this was mostly due to disinterest in the Fiero as per this topic's thread. Almost 200,000 Fieros were sold in the first two years of its existence, and yet GM still pulled the plug as quickly as it could. (I would go through at least two generations of any vehicle before declaring it flatlined. Even the Aztec will get this much TLC from Pontiac.) I would think 200,000 units in 2 years would tell GM that the idea is sound, even if the "engine fire" recall hurt sales. A 2nd gen redesign would solve that, and besides, how many cars roll off the line that have to be recalled? More models have recalls than not.<P>Thanks for everyone's input. I have learned a lot on this forum. (Not to say I am going anywhere anytime soon.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>- Flamberge||||reg||1
